lessons.notes:
## Why generics?

Generics let you write code once and use it with many types, while TypeScript still tracks the exact types. They are "functions for types".

## Generic functions

```typescript
function identity<T>(value: T): T {
  return value;
}
const wrap = <T,>(value: T): T[] => [value];   // arrow form

identity(5);            // T = number (inferred)
identity<string>('a');  // T given explicitly
```

## Generic interfaces, types and classes

```typescript
interface ApiResponse<T> {
  ok: boolean;
  data: T;
}
type UserResponse = ApiResponse<{ name: string }>;

class Stack<T> {
  private items: T[] = [];
  push(item: T): void { this.items.push(item); }
  pop(): T | undefined { return this.items.pop(); }
}
```

Built-in generics you already use: `Array<T>`, `Promise<T>`, `Map<K, V>`, `Set<T>`, `Record<K, V>`.

## Constraints

```typescript
function maxBy<T>(items: T[], score: (item: T) => number): T | undefined {
  let best: T | undefined;
  let bestScore = -Infinity;
  for (const item of items) {
    const s = score(item);
    if (s > bestScore) { best = item; bestScore = s; }
  }
  return best;
}
```

Use `T extends SomeShape` when you need to access properties of `T`.

## Defaults

`type Container<T = string> = { value: T }` uses `string` when no type is given.

## Guidelines

- Use a generic only when it **connects** types (input to output). If `T` appears once, you probably do not need it.
- Prefer letting TypeScript infer type arguments.
- Name type parameters `T`, `K`, `V`, or descriptive names like `TItem` for clarity.

lessons.example:
function groupBy<T, K extends string>(items: T[], key: (item: T) => K): Record<K, T[]> {
  const out = {} as Record<K, T[]>;
  for (const item of items) {
    const k = key(item);
    (out[k] ??= []).push(item);
  }
  return out;
}

const words = ['apple', 'avocado', 'banana', 'blueberry', 'cherry'];
const byLetter = groupBy(words, (w) => w[0]);
for (const [letter, list] of Object.entries(byLetter)) {
  console.log(letter, '->', list.join(', '));
}

