## Fields and constructors

```typescript
class Point {
  x: number;
  y: number;
  constructor(x: number, y: number) {
    this.x = x;
    this.y = y;
  }
  distanceTo(other: Point): number {
    return Math.hypot(this.x - other.x, this.y - other.y);
  }
}
```

With `strict` mode, every field must be definitely assigned (in its declaration or in the constructor).

## Modifiers

| Modifier | Who can access | Enforced |
|---|---|---|
| `public` | everyone | - |
| `protected` | class + subclasses | compile time |
| `private` | the class | compile time |
| `#name` | the class | runtime too |
| `readonly` | read by allowed code, set only in constructor | compile time |
| `static` | accessed on the class itself | - |

## Parameter properties (avoid in this course)

`constructor(private name: string) {}` declares and assigns a field in one go. It generates JavaScript, so it is **not erasable** and does not run with Node's type stripping. Write the field explicitly.

## Interfaces and implements

```typescript
interface Comparable<T> {
  compareTo(other: T): number;
}
class Money implements Comparable<Money> {
  readonly cents: number;
  constructor(cents: number) { this.cents = cents; }
  compareTo(other: Money): number { return this.cents - other.cents; }
}
```

`implements` checks the class; it does not add any code.

## Abstract classes

```typescript
abstract class Report {
  abstract body(): string;
  print(): void { console.log(`== Report ==\n${this.body()}`); }
}
class Sales extends Report {
  body(): string { return 'Sales are up'; }
}
```

Use `override` (with the `noImplicitOverride` option) to mark methods that replace a parent method; it catches typos in method names.

## Classes are types

A class name is also a type: `let p: Point`. Thanks to structural typing, any object with the same public shape is compatible.

