lessons.notes:
## The most useful utility types

| Utility | Result |
|---|---|
| `Partial<T>` | all properties optional |
| `Required<T>` | all properties required |
| `Readonly<T>` | all properties readonly |
| `Pick<T, K>` | only keys K |
| `Omit<T, K>` | all keys except K |
| `Record<K, V>` | object with keys K and values V |
| `Exclude<U, X>` | remove X from union U |
| `Extract<U, X>` | keep only X from union U |
| `NonNullable<T>` | remove `null` and `undefined` |
| `ReturnType<F>` | return type of function type F |
| `Parameters<F>` | parameter types as a tuple |
| `Awaited<P>` | type inside a Promise |

## Examples

```typescript
interface User { id: number; name: string; email: string; password: string }

type PublicUser = Omit<User, 'password'>;
type Login = Pick<User, 'email' | 'password'>;
type UserPatch = Partial<Omit<User, 'id'>>;

type Role = 'admin' | 'editor' | 'guest';
type Staff = Exclude<Role, 'guest'>;     // 'admin' | 'editor'
const perms: Record<Role, string[]> = {
  admin: ['all'], editor: ['write'], guest: [],
};
```

Utilities compose: `Partial<Omit<User, 'id'>>` is "any user fields except id, all optional".

## Mapped types

```typescript
type Getters<T> = {
  [K in keyof T as `get${Capitalize<string & K>}`]: () => T[K];
};
```

Mapped types loop over keys (`[K in keyof T]`), can add or remove `?` and `readonly` (`-?`, `-readonly`), and can rename keys with template literal types.

## Why use them?

- **Single source of truth**: change `User` and every derived type updates.
- **Less duplication**: no hand-copied "almost the same" interfaces.
- **Safer APIs**: `Record<Status, ...>` forces every case to be handled.

