lessons.notes:
## typeof in types

```typescript
const defaults = { volume: 50, muted: false };
type Defaults = typeof defaults;               // { volume: number; muted: boolean }

function createUser(name: string) { return { name, createdAt: new Date() }; }
type User = ReturnType<typeof createUser>;
```

Write the value (or function) once, derive the type.

## keyof

```typescript
type Point = { x: number; y: number };
type Axis = keyof Point;   // 'x' | 'y'

function setAxis(p: Point, axis: Axis, v: number): void {
  p[axis] = v;
}
```

Note: `Object.keys(obj)` returns `string[]`, not `(keyof T)[]`, because objects may have extra properties at runtime.

## Indexed access types

```typescript
type Order = { items: { sku: string; qty: number }[] };
type Item = Order['items'][number];   // { sku: string; qty: number }
type Qty = Item['qty'];               // number
```

## Template literal types

```typescript
type Event = 'click' | 'focus';
type Handler = `on${Capitalize<Event>}`;   // 'onClick' | 'onFocus'
```

Built-in helpers: `Uppercase`, `Lowercase`, `Capitalize`, `Uncapitalize`.

## satisfies

```typescript
type Route = { path: string; auth: boolean };
const routes = {
  home: { path: '/', auth: false },
  admin: { path: '/admin', auth: true },
} satisfies Record<string, Route>;

routes.admin.path;  // OK, keys are known exactly
```

Compared with an annotation (`const routes: Record<string, Route>`), `satisfies` keeps the precise inferred type, so `routes.typo` is still an error.

## Putting it together

A common pattern: a constant object is the single source of truth, and types are derived with `typeof`, `keyof` and indexed access. Change the object and all types follow automatically.

